  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nCancelled the flood watch early for portions of Northern IN as most \nof the high-res guidance has backed off on the higher rainfall \ntotals in those areas. Otherwise, the deformation zone associated \nwith the upper low/trough (which are presently occurring where we \ndidn't get as much precipitation earlier today) moves through. A \nshortwave will drop south across Lake MI/Lower MI around 18-00z \nbefore the upper ridge moves in and puts an end to precip chances. \nSurface winds shift from more northeast to N-NW behind this feature \nnear the lake, with an area of convergence developing. Have pops \npersisting through the day, with around 20-50 percent northwest and \nthen 60-90 percent along/south of US 24. The environment supports \nefficient rain-producing cells, so if there are storms that end up \nredeveloping/training over the same area or there are cells that \nremain fairly stationary for longer periods of time as the broader \nsurface low/trough meanders E-NE, we could end up with some \nlocalized flooding. High-res guidance focuses the best potential in \nNW OH and portions of Northern Indiana. Kept the watch going for the \nIN counties, and then expanded the watch into NW Ohio in \ncollaboration with NWS CLE/DTX. Around an inch or so of \nprecipitation has fallen in many areas within the watch, and an \nadditional 1 to 2 inches is expected through the day (of course, \nlocally higher amounts possible). \n\nWith the trough sinking down this afternoon, present NNE flow shift \nmore N-NW for a period on Lake Michigan. Waves are presently at \naround 2-4 feet, lowest up near South Haven, MI where wave periods \nare about 4 seconds. Further south near Michigan City, waves are \ngreatest with longer periods of around 6 seconds. Yesterday \nafternoon/evening, waves at Michigan city got up to around 5 feet, \nand around 4 feet near Warren Dunes, MI. Northern Berrien (including \nSilver Beach in St. Joseph, MI) and the South Haven area (buoy \n45168) lingered around 2 feet. We are in the 'lull-period' where \nwaves may briefly drop early this morning into the first part of the \nday, but expect them to reach heights of 4 to 7 feet as the flow \nbecomes more onshore (vs somewhat shore parallel). Wave periods \nbegin to lengthen to more like 6-8 seconds by late afternoon and \nearly evening, especially further south. Will persist with the \ncurrent marine headlines where Southern Berrien County, MI and La \nPorte County, IN are in a beach hazards statement all day today and \ncontinuing into Monday morning. Northern Berrien County, MI \n(including Silver Beach in St. Joseph, MI) will be mostly moderate \nswim risk conditions until 5 pm EDT, when winds and waves pick up. \nHave the Beach Hazards Statement starting at that time and \npersisting through Monday morning. The small craft advisory for the \nmarine zones remains the same as well, with winds/waves dropping \nbelow criteria around 5 am EDT. \n\nExpect a strong longshore current moving from N to S along the \nshore, with strong structural currents expected (particularly on the \nnorth sides of piers and breakwalls). Rip currents are expected \ngiven increasing wave heights and lengthening wave periods, \nespecially this afternoon when winds/waves become more directly \nonshore (vs more oblique or shore-parallel as they are this \nmorning). Wave heights diminish late tonight/early Mon AM towards \naround 2-3 feet, but wave periods remain around 6-7 seconds through \nearly Monday afternoon. Kept the BH.S End time at 8 am Mon, with a \nmoderate swim risk forecast for all but northern Berrien County, MI \nduring the day on Monday. By the afternoon, waves should be only \naround 1-2 feet. \n\nMostly dry conditions tonight into Wednesday with an upper \nridge/surface high in place. Highs will be in the upper 70s and low \nto mid 80s, warmest Wednesday. Chances for showers and storms return \nWednesday night and persist into the weekend as a broad upper level \nlow moves across the Canadian Prairie and into Hudson Bay. This will \nbring a trough and cold front into the CWA by Wed night-sinking \nsoutheastward and stalling somewhere across our CWA into Thursday. \nGuidance is all over the place for Friday into the weekend, with \nsome shifting the boundary southeast out of our area and others \nbringing in a secondary trough axis that keeps potential for \nprecipitation going into the weekend. The best potential for \nshowers/storms (30-60 percent) will be Wednesday night into \nThursday, then lower chances (20 to 40 percent) persist into the \nweekend given model discrepancies. Highs will be in the 80s Thursday \ninto the weekend, warmest Sunday.",
